# Google Consent Manager for DokuWiki

Manage optional analytics and advertising consent, conditionally load Google
tags, add webmaster verification metadata, and provide Google News sitemap and
IndexNow integrations.

## Setup

Install the plugin in `lib/plugins/googleconsentmananger/`, then configure it
in DokuWiki's Configuration Manager:

* Set **Consent mode** to `strict` or `consent`.
* Configure a GTM container ID (`GTM-...`), a GA4 measurement ID (`G-...`), or
  an AdSense publisher ID (`ca-pub-` followed by 16 digits), as needed.
* Add optional Google, Bing, Yandex, and Pinterest verification tokens.
* Set the privacy policy URL and consent retention period.
* For a Google News sitemap, set the exact publication name registered with
  Google News, its two-letter language, and the namespace containing articles.
* For IndexNow, enter an 8–128 character alphanumeric/hyphen key and publish
  its matching key response URL as described below.

Invalid IDs and verification tokens are ignored. The privacy policy URL must be
a root-relative path or an HTTP(S) URL. Verification tags are independent from
tracking consent.

## Features and settings

All settings are available in DokuWiki's Configuration Manager:

| Setting | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `consentMode` | `strict` (default) gates all Google tag downloads until consent; `consent` loads GTM before a choice with Google's consent defaults denied. |
| `GTMID` | Optional Google Tag Manager container (`GTM-...`). |
| `GA4ID` | Optional standalone Google Analytics 4 measurement ID (`G-...`); ignored when GTM is configured. |
| `adsenseID` | Optional AdSense Auto Ads publisher ID (`ca-pub-` plus 16 digits). |
| `googleVerification`, `bingVerification`, `yandexVerification`, `pinterestVerification` | Optional search-engine/webmaster domain-verification tokens, emitted as standard meta tags. |
| `newsPublicationName`, `newsLanguage`, `newsNamespace` | Enable a Google News sitemap for a namespace, using the publication's exact registered name and a two-letter language code. |
| `indexNowKey` | Optional IndexNow API key (8–128 letters, digits, or hyphens); enables the verification response URL and page-change notifications. |
| `acceptBody` | Custom consent-banner message. |
| `policyURL` | Optional privacy-policy link in the banner. |
| `expireDays` | Consent-cookie lifetime in days (1–3650; default 180). |
| `acceptOnContinue`, `acceptOnScroll`, `acceptAnyClick` | Optional shortcuts that accept both analytics and advertising; disabled by default. |

The consent interface offers accept-all, reject-optional, and per-category
preferences for analytics and advertising; necessary functionality and security
storage are always allowed. The saved choice can be changed or withdrawn using
the persistent **Cookie preferences** button. The banner uses translated
language strings, supports keyboard focus and Escape to close the preferences
panel, and is shown on page-view actions. Consent is stored separately from
DokuWiki's own session and other cookies.

## Consent behavior

**Strict opt-in** is the default. No GTM, GA4, or AdSense remote script is
requested until the visitor opts into its category. GA4 loads only when
analytics is allowed; Auto Ads loads only when advertising is allowed. If GTM
is configured, it loads after either optional category is accepted. Configure
GTM tags with appropriate built-in Consent Mode checks so tags respect the
visitor's category choices.

**Consent Mode** initializes Google's consent defaults as denied, then loads
the configured GTM container even before a choice is saved. Google tags may
send cookieless pings while consent is denied, depending on the tags and
container configuration. Choose strict opt-in if any pre-consent Google
requests are unacceptable.

Consent is stored in a plugin-specific cookie. Visitors can reopen **Cookie
preferences** to update or withdraw optional consent. Necessary functionality
and security storage remain allowed; analytics and advertising are controlled
separately. A changed choice updates Google Consent Mode immediately, but a
script already downloaded cannot be unloaded from the current page.
Existing `cb-enabled` cookie-bar choices are not migrated because they cannot
represent separate analytics and advertising preferences; visitors will be
asked to make a new choice after upgrading.

The optional accept-on-navigation, accept-on-scroll, and accept-on-click
settings treat those actions as acceptance of **both** optional categories.
These interactions may be accidental and may not constitute valid consent in
your jurisdiction; leave them disabled unless you have reviewed the legal
implications. Scrolling is disabled at `0`; a stored legacy on/off value of
`1` is upgraded to a 1000-pixel threshold to avoid accidental near-immediate
acceptance.

## Integrations and duplication

If both GTM and a GA4 ID are configured, the standalone GA4 tag is not loaded;
configure GA4 in GTM instead. Do not configure the same GA4 property or Auto
Ads integration both directly and in GTM, or duplicate collection/ads may
result. This plugin gates its direct integrations, but third-party scripts
added by the DokuWiki template or other plugins are outside its control. In
strict mode, configure GTM tags to require the matching additional consent
before firing; default Consent Mode checks may allow cookieless pings.
The internal preference-cookie name intentionally follows the existing
`googleconsentmananger` plugin identifier.

The Webmaster-inspired verification fields add standard verification meta
tags. GA4 support follows the measurement-ID setup pattern; Auto Ads support
uses the publisher-ID script pattern. These features were informed by
[dokuwiki-plugin-webmaster](https://github.com/mprins/dokuwiki-plugin-webmaster),
[dokuwiki-plugin-googleanalytics](https://github.com/tatewake/dokuwiki-plugin-googleanalytics),
and [dokuwiki-google-auto-ads-plugin](https://github.com/hbschiller/dokuwiki-google-auto-ads-plugin);
this plugin implements them independently and does not copy their code.

## Google News sitemap

When the publication name, language, and namespace are configured, the plugin
generates a sitemap at `?do=googleconsentnews`. It includes only public,
non-hidden pages in that namespace that were first created within the last
48 hours, newest first, up to Google's 1,000-URL limit. The page's first
heading is used as its title; pages without one use their page name. Submit
this sitemap URL in Search Console. Only use the configured namespace for
actual news articles; Google News inclusion is subject to Google's publisher
policies and is not guaranteed by sitemap submission.

## IndexNow

After page creation, edits, reverts, or deletion, the plugin submits the page
URL to the IndexNow API; hidden pages and pages without public read access are
excluded for non-deletion events. Minor edits are ignored. IndexNow setup requires
the configured key to be verifiable at
`?do=googleconsentindexnowkey`; that URL responds with the key as plain text.
Configure the full HTTPS key-location URL in IndexNow/Search Console tools if
requested. The IndexNow request runs during the page-save request and can take
up to five seconds when the remote service is slow. Pages are still saved if a
submission fails.

## Compatibility

The plugin uses DokuWiki's action-plugin events, Configuration Manager, and
JavaScript information. No external JavaScript library is required beyond
DokuWiki's bundled jQuery for plugin initialization. Test integrations with
your DokuWiki template and any GTM tags before enabling them on a live site.

## Suggested name

**DokuWiki Consent & Search Toolkit** better reflects the combination of
consent management, Google tag integrations, webmaster verification, news
sitemaps, and IndexNow notifications than “Google Consent Manager.” The
existing plugin ID is misspelled (`googleconsentmananger`); renaming the ID or
installation directory is not part of this documentation update because it
would affect upgrades, configuration, and stored consent.

